50291 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351068 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351068 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351068 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186349844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351068 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cef03a78-3898-53d7-a31f-076237c237e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186350763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351293 #edb/ref 1071 "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=3-{x}^{2}$ and $y=x+1,$ then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a289df3d-5c34-5bee-85db-98d71fbc43da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351293 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](images/73/7316d667d7308ac5ec0f5348055d3d0e7eb90b97abaa74f5693c3151dc3b8bb1.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-{x}^{2} & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$-2\\le x\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](images/aa/aab6fb99694b518bfa9d200a75876c9e888ba1ab7d699464bc9caca360be775a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 1,\\,x+1\\le y\\le 3-{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[{\\int }_{x+1}^{3-{x}^{2}}f(x,y)\\text{ }dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351295 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351295 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351295 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351295 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351295 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bba1a6f7-479e-5c7c-a4a7-2bc8097d537b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351295 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3x{e}^{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}3x{e}^{y}\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{e}^{y}\\,dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x{[{e}^{y}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}-{e}^{0})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}3x(x-1)dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(3{x}^{2}-3x)dx & ={[{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(64-24)-(1-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =40+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351369 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186351057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351369 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351369 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351369 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351369 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "adc09746-e915-53ae-9b39-14d8d6a2a18e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351369 #edb/ref 1074 "The integral is defined over the type I region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,2{x}^{2}\\le y\\le {x}^{2}+1}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y$, treating $x$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}x\\,dy\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dy]dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[y]_{2{x}^{2}}^{{x}^{2}+1}dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x({x}^{2}+1-2{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(1-{x}^{2})dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{3})dx & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186408137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1045 "Double Integrals Over Type I Regions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186408133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408118 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "74e0d3a9-0075-5d33-b25c-7e801fde9553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408119 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408119 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/r